# Complete multiple household tasks

**Reference pattern.** These examples are local design guidance. They do not install new Home Assistant screens or perform device actions.

A task list describes work to finish, not every feature on the dashboard. It is useful when setup has several independent parts and people cannot complete them in one sitting.

## Progress is a composition, not an automation

Use [question pages](question-pages.html#compose-the-task-without-replacing-its-owner) for each input and its retained answers. This pattern owns the progress view and dependencies across those tasks. The [task-list component](../components/task-list.html) owns the task-row presentation.

Opening or completing a documentation task must not enable an automation. Keep a saved draft, a reviewed setup and an enabled native rule as separate facts. Route to an actual supported editor only when the installation provides it; otherwise state that the example is a reference and preserve the implementation gap.

## The NHS principle we adapt

The [NHS multiple tasks pattern](https://service-manual.nhs.uk/design-system/patterns/complete-multiple-tasks) groups related work, exposes progress and distinguishes incomplete work from dependencies. civica applies that structure to longer household configuration. Simplify the task before adding a progress screen.

## Start with a small status vocabulary

Use “Completed” and “Incomplete” where possible. Add “Cannot start yet” only for a genuine dependency and explain what unlocks it. Keep a completed task available for correction. A disabled task name should be plain text rather than a link that does nothing.

