## Draft, complete and enabled are different

A saved quiet-hours draft is not a working notification rule. Count a task as complete using a clear product rule, or let the person explicitly say they have finished when human review is needed. Enabling the resulting configuration is a separate, named action. Returning users should see their saved work, the current dependency state and any changed devices.

<%= example('nhs-tasks-resume-draft') %>

## Repair without discarding progress

Use a problem state only when there is a problem to fix. “Sensor unavailable” is not evidence that the user answered a question incorrectly. Distinguish a temporarily unavailable integration from a permanently removed selection. Preserve unaffected task answers and explain whether the person can continue.

<%= example('nhs-tasks-repair') %>

## Accessibility and content

Write task names as household activities: “Choose household events”, not “Configure trigger schema”. Associate each task link with its text status. Group a large setup under meaningful headings. Do not rely on a coloured badge, a completion tick or a progress percentage alone. Let rows wrap at small widths and keep task names before their status in reading order.

If completion depends on optional notes or a discussion with someone else, ask whether the task is finished. Avoid guessed durations and countdowns. Keep saved-progress messages distinct from device notifications so the page does not announce every background entity update.

## Home Assistant implementation boundary

Persist draft answers and completion state deliberately; do not infer a saved draft from a partially rendered UI. Re-evaluate capability-dependent tasks when integrations change. A task-list link must open a real supported configuration route. The examples are local demonstrations: they do not discover meters, enable automations or grant notification access.
